from beliefs_a import C
C("cantanswer","Selling","If they ask something I can't answer, I'm finished.",
 "You imagine the one question that exposes you, and the silence after it.",
 ["Nobody knows every answer. Professionals are the ones who handle not-knowing calmly.","'Bra fråga – jag vill ge dig ett korrekt svar, så jag kollar och mejlar dig i dag' builds trust: it shows you won't make things up.",
  "Then actually send the answer the same day. That follow-through is more impressive than a guess.","The questions repeat. After 5 calls you'll have heard most of them."],
 "Not knowing is normal. Checking and coming back the same day is professional.",
 ["Pause. Breathe.","Say the 'jag kollar' line.","Write the question down in front of them.","Answer by email the same day.","Add the question to your list of 10."],
 dict(type="fear",fear="One question ends everything",reality="'Jag kollar och återkommer i dag' → trust goes up"),
 "Write your 'jag kollar' sentence and say it out loud twice.",
 dict(title="When you don't know (Swedish)",lines=["Bra fråga. Jag vill inte gissa, så jag kollar och mejlar dig svaret i dag.","[Skriv ner frågan.]","Var det något mer du undrade över?"]))
C("alreadyhave","Selling","When they say 'vi har redan någon' it's over.",
 "It sounds like a final no, and you don't want to seem pushy.",
 ["Often it's a reflex answer, not a decision. It can mean: we have someone, we're busy, or we're not sure we need it.","You don't need to argue. You can ask one respectful question and offer the free report anyway — it lets them check their current setup.",
  "If they're happy with their provider: great, thank them and move on. That's a fit question, not a failure."],
 "'Vi har redan någon' is information. One calm question and a free second opinion is enough.",
 ["Thank them.","Ask one question: 'Är ni nöjda med hur ni syns i kartan i dag?'","Offer the report as a free second opinion.","Accept whatever they say."],
 dict(type="scale",a="'Vi har redan någon' = rejection",b="'Vi har redan någon' = maybe; offer a free second opinion"),
 "Write your one-question response to 'vi har redan någon'.",
 dict(title="Reply to 'vi har redan någon' (Swedish)",lines=["Vad bra att ni har det täckt.","Får jag fråga – är ni nöjda med hur ni syns i Google-kartan just nu?","Rapporten är gratis, så den kan fungera som en second opinion till den ni redan har. Vill du ha den ändå?"]))
C("followup","Selling","Following up is pushy.",
 "No reply feels like 'no', and a second email feels like ignoring their answer.",
 ["Silence usually isn't an answer. Busy owners miss emails all the time.","Woodpecker's follow-up data and Backlinko's 12M-email study both show that follow-ups bring a large share of replies.",
  "Pushy = many follow-ups with no new value. Polite = 1–2 follow-ups, each with ONE new useful thing, and an easy 'no'.","Ending with 'säg bara till så hör jag inte av mig igen' makes it safe for both of you."],
 "One or two kind follow-ups, each with something new and an easy no, is normal business manners.",
 ["Wait 3–4 working days.","Find one new observation about their profile.","Write 2–3 sentences.","Add the easy-no line.","After the 2nd follow-up, stop."],
 dict(type="ladder",steps=["Email 1: observation + free report","Wait 3–4 days","Follow-up 1: one new fact","Wait 5–7 days","Follow-up 2: easy no, then stop"]),
 "Write one follow-up for an email you sent this week — with one new fact.",
 dict(title="Follow-up email (Swedish)",lines=["Hej Anna, en sak till: ni har 6 omdömen från i höstas som inte fått svar.","Det är en av de tre sakerna i rapporten. Ska jag skicka den?","Är det inte aktuellt, säg bara till så hör jag inte av mig igen."]),
 src=("woodpecker_fu","backlinko"))
C("closefirst","Selling","I have to close the deal on the first call.",
 "You think a 'good salesperson' gets the yes immediately, so anything else feels like failing.",
 ["For a 4,900 kr decision, many owners want to think or talk to a partner. That's normal.","A good call ends with a clear next step, not necessarily a signature: 'Ska jag skicka offerten i kväll och så hörs vi på torsdag?'",
  "Pressure lowers trust. Calm clarity raises it.","Your job on the call: understand, explain, agree on the next step."],
 "A call succeeds when we agree on a clear next step. The yes can come later.",
 ["Before the call, decide the next step you'll suggest.","At the end, summarise in one sentence.","Suggest the next step with a day.","Send what you promised the same day."],
 dict(type="ladder",steps=["Understand their situation","Explain the offer in 2 sentences","Agree on next step + day","Send what you promised","Follow up on the day"]),
 "Write the closing sentence you'll use at the end of your next call.",
 dict(title="Closing calmly (Swedish)",lines=["Så om jag sammanfattar: profilen saknar rätt kategori, bilder och svar på omdömen, och du vill synas bättre för ”tandläkare Solna”.","Det jag gör är att fixa det inom 7 arbetsdagar för 4 900 kr, med pengarna-tillbaka-garanti.","Vill du att vi kör, eller vill du fundera till torsdag så ringer jag då?"]))
C("introvert","Self","I'm not a salesperson type.",
 "You picture sales as loud, extroverted and pushy — and you're not that.",
 ["Your selling is mostly written: cold emails and a report. Writing rewards careful, quiet people.","On calls, listening and asking good questions matter more than talking. Many calm, thoughtful people are good at that.",
  "You don't have to become a different person. You need a process: prepare, ask, explain, next step."],
 "I sell my way: prepared, written-first, calm, curious.",
 ["Lean on writing: email + report do most of the work.","On calls, ask more than you talk.","Use your script as a safety net."],
 dict(type="scale",a="Sales = loud and pushy",b="Sales = prepared, curious, clear next step"),
 "Write down 3 strengths you have that help in YOUR kind of selling.")
C("unhappy","Delivery","If a client is unhappy, it proves I'm a fraud.",
 "One bad experience feels like it would cancel everything you've built.",
 ["Every business has unhappy customers sometimes. What matters is how you handle it.","You built the answer in already: a refund guarantee. Using it isn't failure — it's keeping your word.",
  "An unhappy client gives you the most useful feedback you'll get. Ask what exactly disappointed them and fix that for the next client.",
  "Self-compassion research (Breines & Chen, 2012) found people who treated a mistake kindly were more motivated to improve than those who beat themselves up."],
 "An unhappy client is a problem to handle well, not a verdict on me. I keep my promise and learn.",
 ["Listen fully.","Ask: 'Vad exakt motsvarade inte förväntningarna?'","Offer a fix or the refund.","Write down one change to your process."],
 dict(type="fear",fear="One unhappy client = I'm a fraud",reality="Handled well: promise kept, process improved, reputation intact"),
 "Write the 3 sentences you'd say to an unhappy client.",
 dict(title="Unhappy client (Swedish)",lines=["Tack för att du säger det rakt ut.","Vad exakt motsvarade inte det du förväntade dig?","Jag kan antingen fixa det inom två dagar, eller så får du pengarna tillbaka som utlovat. Vad föredrar du?"]),
 src=("selfcomp",))
C("metaads","Delivery","Meta ads are too complicated — I'll break the rules.",
 "Clinic ads have health rules, and you've heard of ads being rejected.",
 ["The rules are written down publicly in Meta's Health and Wellness ad standards. The core ideas fit on a card: no negative self-image, no unqualified timed result promises, careful with before/after.",
  "In Sweden, prescription drugs (like botulinum toxin, 'Botox') can't be advertised to the public — Läkemedelsverket explains this. So you write about the consultation, not the drug.",
  "Your writing checker flags the most common risks. Use it on every ad.","Half true: you will get an ad rejected at some point. You fix it and resubmit — that's normal."],
 "I follow a short, written rule set and check every ad. A rejected ad is a fix, not a failure.",
 ["Read Meta's Health & Wellness page once.","Write your 5-rule card.","Run every ad through the checker.","If rejected: read the reason, fix, resubmit."],
 dict(type="ladder",steps=["Read the policy once","Make a 5-rule card","Write the ad","Run the checker","Fix and publish"]),
 "Write your 5-rule card for clinic ads (use the checker's policy rules as a start).",
 src=("meta_health",))
C("casestudies","Selling","I can't charge before I have case studies.",
 "Everyone online shows screenshots of results. You have none yet, so charging feels like lying.",
 ["You can't claim results you don't have — and you shouldn't. But you can charge for a clearly defined job with a guarantee.",
  "Your proof right now is your preparation: the free report shows the client exactly what you see and what you'll fix.",
  "Bryan Harris got work by making a demo for the company first — a sample is proof (as published in Woodpecker's ebook).",
  "After your first 2–3 clients, you'll have real before/after profiles. Ask permission to show them."],
 "My proof today is the free report and the guarantee. Case studies come from doing the work.",
 ["Make the report excellent.","Charge for the defined job.","Ask every client for permission to use before/after.","Save every screenshot."],
 dict(type="ladder",steps=["Free report (proof of thinking)","First paid job + guarantee","Before/after screenshots","Ask for a review","Case study #1"]),
 "Improve one section of your Google-kartan report template today.",
 src=("hopkins",))
C("silence","Selling","Silence after my email means they hated it.",
 "No reply leaves a gap, and your mind fills it with the worst story.",
 ["Backlinko's 12-million-email study: about 8.5% of outreach emails got any reply. Silence is what happens to most emails — including good ones.",
  "They may not have seen it, may be busy, may forward it later.","You can't read silence. You can only send a polite follow-up and then move on."],
 "Silence is the default, not a message. I follow up once or twice and keep sending.",
 ["Notice the story you're telling.","Replace it: 'Most emails get no reply. That's normal.'","Schedule the follow-up.","Write the next email."],
 dict(type="fear",fear="No reply = they hate it",reality="No reply = normal (most emails get none); follow up, keep going"),
 "Schedule follow-ups for every email you sent this week.",
 src=("backlinko",))
